+
+### Last-program and specialization caches retain objects after teardown
+
+- **Symptom:** the last looked-up or drawn program, its shaders and their constant/argument buffers survive `dm_gfx_teardown`. Border-specialization descriptor keys and records can also survive. Restarting the backend without a raw framework reset can return the previous program when its descriptor address, serial and serial-table generation repeat.
+- **Cause:** `prog_get` and `draw_` held separate function-local strong program references that clearing the serial owner table and shader cache could not release. `g_fn_off` separately held descriptor keys and positive or negative specialization records after `g_fn4` was cleared. The iOS shell's raw data restore does not execute ARC releases, so losing those pointers leaks ownership. Reuse of the old pointer across that raw restore is not established.
+- **Fix:** expose the two program references and their lookup/pass metadata to teardown, clear them after submit, encode and pipeline work drains, and release `g_fn_off` under its lock. Other pipeline, library and feedback caches are outside this change.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=<id> check_t_session_programs`, on a Mac with Metal API Validation. Each case starts the production backend twice without a simulated shell reset. Weak references check program, shader, buffer, descriptor and specialization release. Lookup cases reuse descriptor addresses, serials and generations and check serial-table replacement. Rendering draws red pixels in the first session and green in the second, with and without the encode thread, using the production draw and submission paths. The two shaders share a 4 MiB constant bank and 4 MiB argument arena, counted once per object. These 8 MiB are fixture storage, not measured Zelda retention. The simple shaders have no argument encoder, so their argument-buffer field is attached explicitly to production arena storage. Border cases exercise both supported and unsupported specializations and a fresh map in the second session.
+- **Found on:** while following up [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md#found-on-this-game)'s iPad investigation, reproduced with synthetic programs on an M4 Max Mac. No new iPad test or game run was performed for this change. It does not reproduce or establish the cause of [the residency-commit kernel wait](rendering.md#a-residency-commit-does-not-return-on-an-ipad), the later slow exit, or the game's measured memory growth between sessions. Mapped game-library retention and UIKit/`SegSnap` execution are not covered.
